T*
(Everyone knows how to read a book. Beginning at the first page, you read each word in order 1 ,) Tj
T*
(stopping periodically to think, until you arrive at the last word on the last page. That?s how) Tj
T*
(you read a newspaper article, or a poem, or an email. Why would reading code be any different?) Tj
T*
(Why code is different English text is designed to be read in order. In fact, there are almost) Tj
T*
(no constraints on the order of a text aside from how you want the reader to consume it. In) Tj
T*
(writing this post, I could put the ideas I want to convey in any order I like. Code, on the) Tj
T*
(other hand, is designed to be run by a computer. The order is thus primarily determined by) Tj
T*
(non-human factors. I cannot simply move 2 a line of code to the beginning of a file or function) Tj
T*
(because I think it provides a better introduction to the program for human readers. The other) Tj
T*
(big difference is that English text is always read as a final product, while code is usually) Tj
T*
(read as a diff . We software engineers spend most of our time reading subtle changes to) Tj
T*
(existing code, not brand-new programs. Imagine if reading this post was like that. You would) Tj
T*
(read each successive draft in the order I wrote them, consuming the post as a cha...) Tj
